00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.780
Right. So how do we do this?

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.680
How do we change these
components of a shiny app?

00:07:55.325 --> 00:07:55.565
Well,

00:07:55.565 --> 00:07:57.965
there's one package in
particular that we're going to

00:07:57.965 --> 00:08:01.565
use to do most of these
tasks, and that package is called the

00:08:01.565 --> 00:08:02.785
bslib package.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.770
So bslib stands for
Bootstrap Library,

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:09.810
and Bootstrap is a very popular

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:14.815
framework for CSS and
HTML development. And every shiny

00:08:14.815 --> 00:08:17.715
app is boot built on top
of the bootstrap framework.

00:08:18.895 --> 00:08:22.910
What bslibdoes is it provides the
most new and up to date

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:26.670
bootstrap features in an art
package so our users can take

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:27.890
advantage of those features.

00:08:28.545 --> 00:08:32.225
It works seamlessly with shiny
apps because it's developed by

00:08:32.225 --> 00:08:33.285
the shiny team.

00:08:33.905 --> 00:08:37.025
It also works with our markdown
documents and even quarto

00:08:37.025 --> 00:08:40.690
documents because those documents
are also built upon Bootstrap.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:47.070
In fact, bslib is so integrated
into shiny that is imported by

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.935
the shiny package. So if
you've installed the shiny package,

00:08:49.935 --> 00:08:52.995
you already have it and you could
start to use it in your app.

00:08:53.535 --> 00:08:55.235
And that's what I'll do here.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:58.970
I will tell the app that we're
going to start using some

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:01.230
functions that come
from the bslib package,

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:03.465
do that with library bslib,

00:09:04.105 --> 00:09:06.905
And then the first functions
we'll use will recreate the app

00:09:06.905 --> 00:09:08.345
page for us.

00:09:08.345 --> 00:09:11.145
Now I said that I really
liked the sidebar layout and I meant

00:09:11.145 --> 00:09:13.970
it and that's what I want
to create, a page with the side

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:15.810
bar. Typically in shiny,

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.410
you might do that by
calling fluidPage,

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:21.665
giving a title panel,
you'll call side bar layout,

00:09:21.665 --> 00:09:24.865
and give that a sidebar
panel, and also a main panel,

00:09:24.865 --> 00:09:26.885
and you'll put your
contents inside of there.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.980
BSLid provides a slightly more efficient
way to create a sidebar app.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:34.015
So let's take a look.

00:09:35.295 --> 00:09:39.075
We'll use the function page
sidebar, which comes in BSlib.

00:09:39.935 --> 00:09:41.555
It takes a title argument

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:44.460
We don't need to use
the Title Panel helper.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:46.800
We could just pass the
title straight to that argument.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:49.840
So I'll copy and paste
that from over here.

00:09:50.835 --> 00:09:52.935
And next it takes
a sidebar argument,

00:09:55.235 --> 00:09:59.255
and we use a helper
function called sidebar with that argument.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.640
Whatever we put inside sidebar
will appear in the sidebar

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:07.080
and I have this object
called sidebar content

00:10:08.025 --> 00:10:09.145
We'll look at it in a second,

00:10:09.145 --> 00:10:11.245
but it contains everything
I want in the sidebar.

00:10:12.025 --> 00:10:14.185
And then finally,
to use page sidebar,

00:10:14.185 --> 00:10:15.950
anything you want
in the main panel,

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:18.690
you just list as
additional arguments outside of sidebar.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:22.370
So we put everything
else here. Literally.

00:10:23.285 --> 00:10:24.905
Now if we run this app,

00:10:26.485 --> 00:10:29.685
we'll see what we get. So
this is our sidebar app,

00:10:29.685 --> 00:10:32.640
and one thing I want you to
notice right off the bat is The

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.520
styling for this sidebar
app is a little more modern,

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:39.360
a little more up to date versus
the typical shiny sidebar app. For example,

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.195
we have a built in widget
to expand and close the side bar,

00:10:43.195 --> 00:10:46.235
and you can see other
styling differences as well.

00:10:46.235 --> 00:10:48.875
So where did these things come
from? If anyone's curious,

00:10:48.875 --> 00:10:51.490
these are three
widgets and some text,

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:55.410
and I saved them as a list in
a helper file called setup.R,

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.710
that my app is sourcing.

00:10:57.225 --> 00:11:00.025
So down here, you see an
object called sidebar content,

00:11:00.025 --> 00:11:01.165
which is a list.

00:11:02.025 --> 00:11:04.880
This idea of grouping widgets
together in list is a really

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:07.760
helpful pattern if you plan to
use the same set of widgets in

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:10.960
multiple places throughout a shiny
app or across shiny apps.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:14.385
I'm just using it today to
keep screen real estate focused on

00:11:14.385 --> 00:11:16.305
the things we're actually
going to be touching.

00:11:16.305 --> 00:11:18.565
So that's where sidebar
content comes from.

00:11:19.105 --> 00:11:21.310
Now everything
else I just typed,

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:23.230
but that's not really
what we want in our app.

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:24.690
We want these things

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:27.630
from our old app over here.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:30.595
Let's pull just a couple of
them into our app so we could

00:11:30.595 --> 00:11:33.575
start work with laying them out
before the screen gets too crowded.

00:11:34.355 --> 00:11:37.415
So if I put things
into my page sidebar,

00:11:38.732 --> 00:11:42.790
bslib or shiny,
we'll add them one

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:46.170
beneath the other as if each new
item was placed in a new row.

00:11:46.985 --> 00:11:49.305
And if I continue to do
that with all the items I have,

00:11:49.305 --> 00:11:51.065
I'll be back at
that endless scroll,

00:11:51.065 --> 00:11:52.905
which is what I'm
trying to avoid.

00:11:52.905 --> 00:11:55.305
Really what I want to do
is place the items beside each

00:11:55.305 --> 00:11:58.220
other as if each new
item was a new column,

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.880
and there's a function in bslib
that allows me to do that.

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:03.260
It's called layout_columns.

00:12:04.415 --> 00:12:06.675
Let's add that here and
see what happens. Call

00:12:08.602 --> 00:12:12.390
layout_columns and
you give it as much

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:13.690
content as you want.

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:16.650
I close the parentheses.

00:12:18.775 --> 00:12:21.995
Now if I run the
app, those items

00:12:22.775 --> 00:12:24.315
are organized into columns.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:29.040
So this is good.
This is a start,

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:31.500
but we can see a
couple things here.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:35.165
The first thing I see is These
are four different items,

00:12:35.165 --> 00:12:38.205
but the character strings are
really just titles for the

00:12:38.205 --> 00:12:40.365
plots, and they should
probably travel together.

00:12:40.365 --> 00:12:42.370
I don't want them
in separate columns,

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:45.250
I want each title and
plot in a single column,

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:47.590
and then the next title
and plot and so on.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:49.010
In a shiny app,

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:51.330
you might collect these things
together with something like a

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:54.995
well panel, but bslib
introduces a new concept called

00:12:54.995 --> 00:12:57.555
a card that I'm
going to use here.

00:12:57.555 --> 00:13:00.210
If I want to collect two
things together in a card,

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:02.950
I surround them in
the function card.

00:13:09.235 --> 00:13:12.755
And then if I want the card
to have a title or a header,

00:13:12.755 --> 00:13:15.495
I would wrap that in
another function called card header.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:19.170
The reason for that is it'll
make it easier for us to style

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:21.110
that header a little later on.

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:23.510
So go ahead and do that.

00:13:28.235 --> 00:13:30.255
And then we can take a
look at what we have.

00:13:31.915 --> 00:13:32.635
Alright,

00:13:32.635 --> 00:13:36.040
so now our apps and our
titles are bound together visually in

00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.680
a way that I think
looks really attractive.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.000
